RESET has to restore CPUID.01H:EDX.APIC, not only IA32_APIC_BASE.EN.

A guest that switches its local APIC off through IA32_APIC_BASE (Windows 98
does, on the way out of a session) makes apic_set_base() clear the enable
bit *and* the CPUID feature bit -- correct, that is what the hardware does.
RESET puts the enable bit back in apic_reset_common() but nothing ever puts
the feature bit back, so the next boot runs on a CPU that reports no local
APIC while the APIC is enabled again. SeaBIOS then prints "No apic - only
the main cpu is present", skips smp_setup() and never programs LINT0 as
ExtINT; pic_irq_request() meanwhile sees an enabled APIC and routes the
i8259's output into it, where apic_accept_pic_intr() refuses it because
LINT0 is masked. Every PIC interrupt is dropped: the guest hangs on the
first BIOS-tick wait of its boot (Win98's IO.SYS, or the countdown of an
install CD's boot menu) with the timer IRQ pending in the 8259 for ever.

Reproduced on QEMU 9.2.4 and, unchanged, on a stock 11.1.0. The guard is
tools/win98-reboot-test.sh; the patch README row has the whole story.

diff --git a/hw/intc/apic_common.c b/hw/intc/apic_common.c
index 49c03a5bce..b0eec5c5b5 100644
--- a/hw/intc/apic_common.c
+++ b/hw/intc/apic_common.c
@@ -239,6 +239,20 @@ static void apic_reset_common(DeviceState *dev)
     s->apicbase = APIC_DEFAULT_ADDRESS | bsp | MSR_IA32_APICBASE_ENABLE;
     s->id = s->initial_apic_id;
 
+    /*
+     * The enable bit is back (line above), so the CPUID feature bit that
+     * follows it on real hardware has to come back too: apic_set_base()
+     * clears CPUID.01H:EDX.APIC when a guest switches the APIC off through
+     * IA32_APIC_BASE, and nothing else ever sets it again. Leaving it clear
+     * across a reset tells the next boot's firmware that this CPU has no
+     * local APIC, so it never programs LINT0 as ExtINT -- while the enable
+     * bit being set again routes the i8259's output through the APIC, whose
+     * masked LINT0 then drops every PIC interrupt on the floor.
+     */
+    if (s->cpuid_apic) {
+        cpu_set_apic_feature(&s->cpu->env);
+    }
+
     kvm_reset_irq_delivered();
 
     s->vapic_paddr = 0;
@@ -279,6 +293,9 @@ static void apic_common_realize(DeviceState *dev, Error **errp)
         return;
     }
 
+    /* what the CPU model advertises, for apic_reset_common() to restore */
+    s->cpuid_apic = !!(s->cpu->env.features[FEAT_1_EDX] & CPUID_APIC);
+
     /* Note: We need at least 1M to map the VAPIC option ROM */
     if (!vapic && s->vapic_control & VAPIC_ENABLE_MASK &&
             current_machine->ram_size >= 1024 * 1024) {
diff --git a/include/hw/i386/apic_internal.h b/include/hw/i386/apic_internal.h
index a26d53bb9d..3169a9908b 100644
--- a/include/hw/i386/apic_internal.h
+++ b/include/hw/i386/apic_internal.h
@@ -188,6 +188,10 @@ struct APICCommonState {
     DeviceState *vapic;
     hwaddr vapic_paddr; /* note: persistence via kvmvapic */
     uint32_t extended_log_dest;
+    /* CPUID.01H:EDX.APIC as the CPU model configures it: a guest that turns
+     * the APIC off through IA32_APIC_BASE clears the feature bit with the
+     * enable bit, and RESET has to bring both back (apic_reset_common). */
+    bool cpuid_apic;
 };
 
